    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d264">

                <pb facs="0561" n="531" sameAs="frus:frus1958-60v18/pg531"/>

                <head>264. Telegram From the Embassy in Korea to the Department of State<anchor
                        n="0" xml:id="d264-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Seoul</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1959-01-16T12:00:00+08:30">January 16, 1959,
                            noon</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>321. I saw President Rhee yesterday to say goodbye and in response his indication
                    few days ago that he wanted to talk with me. Conversation was friendly except
                    for one moment when I questioned his assertion Democratic Party was, in effect,
                    Communist organization.</p>
                <p>President Rhee, while declaring his attachment to democratic principles, said
                    twice during conversation that in struggle against Communist infiltration and
                    subversion, some departure from democratic processes would be necessary. He
                    assured me he welcomed my comment and advice, but when I urged again that effort
                    be made to restore normal working relationship between parties, came up with
                    argument re Communist character of DP. In reply my statement to contrary, he
                    implied that if my advice were based on this premise, he would have to disregard
                    it.</p>
                <p>Meeting ended with President urging me to return soon.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">Dowling</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
